1. sys.exit raises SystemExit, which a handler can catch
   try: sys.exit() except SystemExit as e: ...; print("still running")
      stdout: caught SystemExit: code None
      stdout: still running
      exit status 0
2. except Exception does not see it
   try: sys.exit(3) except Exception: print("caught"); print("after")
      exit status 3
3. the exit status: an Integer -- and True is 1, False is 0
   sys.exit(3)
      exit status 3
   sys.exit(True)
      exit status 1
   sys.exit(False)
      exit status 0
4. atexit handlers run in reverse order of registration
   three atexit.register calls, then print("main done")
      stdout: main done
      stdout: third registered
      stdout: second registered
      stdout: first registered
      exit status 0
5. os._exit skips them
   atexit.register(...); os._exit(4)
      exit status 4
6. sys.exit with a string writes it to stderr, runs atexit, and exits 1
   atexit.register(...); sys.exit("fatal: giving up")
      stdout: atexit ran
      stderr (last line): fatal: giving up
      exit status 1
7. an uncaught exception prints a traceback and exits 1; atexit still runs, but sys.exception() is gone
   atexit.register(...); print("before"); raise RuntimeError("boom")
      stdout: before
      stdout: atexit sees sys.exception(): None
      stderr (last line): RuntimeError: boom
      exit status 1
8. KeyboardInterrupt is a BaseException; an uncaught one ends the process by the signal
   raise KeyboardInterrupt, caught as BaseException
      stdout: KeyboardInterrupt < BaseException
      exit status 0
   a real SIGINT, not caught: os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGINT); time.sleep(5)
      stderr (last line): KeyboardInterrupt
      killed by signal 2
   a real SIGINT, caught as KeyboardInterrupt
      stdout: caught KeyboardInterrupt
      exit status 0
9. this program's own atexit handler prints last
